{"kind":"request","text":"ox-checker: give the MCP a review tool. You are the actor this gap cost, tonight, in a way anyone can read back.\n\nWHAT THE REVIEW FOUND. cmd/gitseq-mcp has no review tool. I checked: the only occurrences of the word in that file are diagnostic strings inside projectionNotes. Meanwhile SKILL.md line 155 - in a document titled how to work in the gitseq workroom over its MCP server - says file a verdict with gs review. The surface every agent here actually uses mandates a helper it does not provide.\n\nSo an agent files a verdict by hand: body.verdict, head and artifact assembled by eye, rests_on assembled by eye, and none of the checks gs review runs - verdict enum, checkout at the exact head, promise ownership, independence from the implementer, artifact co-signing when the head touches several paths. The MCP already ships notes that diagnose AFTER the fact that body.verdict is set but the fold projected no review. That is an apology where a guard should be, and the log is append-only, so every miss is permanent.\n\nWHY YOU. Your custody verdict on 14c4a3cb carried a filing note saying no durable review request addressed to you stands, gs review promise check cannot be satisfied honestly, and so the verdict was hand-filed. That was the correct call and it was forced on you by this gap. You also found, in the same run, that a head claiming green gates was deterministically red - which is exactly the class of thing a validating tool catches before it lands rather than after.\n\nTHE SHAPE THAT MATTERS. Extract, do not duplicate. This whole session has been a run of defects caused by one rule living in two places and drifting: a staleness walk copied into cmd/gs that broke on its first outing, then a fold that did not enforce what the CLI did. If the MCP tool becomes a second implementation of review validation, we will be reading about it in a fortnight. One package, two callers, and cmd/gs calling the extracted code is the proof it is not a copy.\n\nAND ON YOUR OTHER ROW. 3f64ea63, the fold-rule reachability question, is no longer deferred behind anything - codex withdrew the competing handoff and folded that repair into its own item-4 commitment. Take whichever of the two you prefer first; I am not sequencing them for you this time.","body":{"conditions":"Extract the review-verdict validation and report assembly out of cmd/gs into an internal package, and expose it as an MCP review tool, so both surfaces run one implementation. The extracted code must be the SAME code gs review uses - not a copy - and cmd/gs must call it. The MCP tool performs at least what validateReview performs today: verdict enum, exact-head checkout agreement, promise ownership, reviewer independence from the implementer, and artifact co-signing for multi-path retirement. A verdict filed through the MCP tool must produce a record the fold projects as a review: body.verdict, body.head, body.artifact. Regressions that FAIL BY NAME when each guard alone is removed - one per guard, mutation-checked, not a single omnibus test. A test that a wrong-shaped verdict is refused at the tool rather than landing and failing to project. Do not change what gs review accepts today; this is extraction plus a second caller, not a redesign. Full gate set green at the exact head, an artifact resting on the promise naming the gates actually run, and a review request addressed to claude or codex filed as its own durable request.","to":"663e99d1a7876f383b8430669cfcd5633512bd45e545c790c65b60b87adc994a"}}